I agree. More generally, you want to control for response biases. To me
this would be the starting point:

L2 gender guess == masculine ~ I(L1 Southern gender == masculine) * I(L2
gender == masculine) * (L1 variety of speaker == southern) + (1 + I(L1
Southern gender == masculine) * I(L2 gender == masculine) | participant) +
(1 + I(L1 variety of speaker == southern) | word)

This rules out biases based on the word, overall response biases, etc. It
is conservative and might not converge depending on how much data you have.
You'd be predicting

   - a main effect of L2 gender (e.g., capturing the idea that participants
   might have SOME knowledge about L2)
   - an interaction between L1 southern gender and L1 variety of the
   speaker (if the answer to your RG is affirmative)
   - potentially an interaction between L2 gender and L1 variety (if
   northerners indeed know more about L2, as you hypothesize below).
   - There might be additional biases that come out as e.g., a 3-way
   interaction.

You could simplify this model to remove the 3-way, but if you can afford it
(as simulations can test), I wouldn't since the above is your full design.
Slightly better would be simplifications in the random effect structure. in
particular, you could remove that L2 gender == masculine by-subject slope
(and with it the interaction), if removing random correlations isn't
sufficient.

I realize that this model is more complex what you suggested but coding
correct or congruent without including the full factorial *does* make some
assumptions (and especially since your design isn't balanced with regard to
L2 gender, these assumptions could get tricky, though perhaps you've
thought through them).

> But I’ve been thinking that an alternative or additional explanation for a
> larger congruency effect for Southerners (or a Region*substandard gender
> interaction) might be that (a) something about the stimuli (all
> monomorphemic, monosyllabic and inanimate) nonetheless says ‘masculine’ or
> ‘feminine’ to Northerners and Southerners alike, too, along the same lines
> as the Southern substandard distinction but (b) Northerners know more about
> L2 and therefore ignore point (a). To rule this latter explanation out as
> best I might, some measure of the participants’ L2 knowledge seemed useful.
>
yes. you could include that or rather test whether inclusion in the above
model (along with at least the interaction of L2 knowledge with L2 gender,
which should be positive)

> The task also contains 15 L2 stimuli whose L1 cognates are neuter in both
> the standard and the substandard. For five of these stimuli, the L2 gender
> is neuter, for six it is masculine, for four it is feminine (inanimate
> feminine L2 words with neuter L1 cognates are few and far between,
> unfortunately). These are all fairly transparent cognates, so I presume
> that most participants will ‘know’ these words receptively. My reasoning
> was that participants with little to no L2 knowledge will essentially
> pursue one of these three strategies: (a) random guessing; (b) tick neuter
> everywhere (transfer); (c) tick another response everywhere (e.g.
> masculinisation strategy). Either strategy should result in a fairly low
> score compared to participants with more extensive L2 knowledge. True,
> strategy (b) would be transfer-based, but not uniquely substandard-based
> (the strategy is available to both Northerners and Southerners).
>
> @Florian: Does that seem reasonable?
>
hmm, I see, but this assumes that ONLY the cognates gender affects
reasoning in this task, rather than e.g., knowledge about the gender
possibilities. at the very least, it seems to me that your transfer
hypothesis would imply that Southerners will more often NOT guess neuter
(i.e., have more variability). I guess as long as this is random, it
doesn't introduce a bias in terms of the mean between Southeners and
Northeners, but wouldn't it mean that some Southerns would look like high
performers, where all Northeners would cluster around 33% correct (prior to
them indeed being more knowledgeable about L2)?

> I do have an alternative, but less direct L2 proficiency measure at my
> disposal: self-assessments on a CEFR-based scale. I guess a cloze test or a
> vocabulary task with non-cognates may be alternatives, but these, too, are
> indirect. The test will be run online, so I’m doing all I can not to scare
