> seeing cognate relationships were there are none, especially if many
> cognates occur elsewhere in the task, so what I intend as non-cognates may
> not be non-cognates to the participants.) The suggestions about non-linear
> proficiency effects and word frequency are excellent, but seeing as writing
> up the analysis without the data at hand is proving difficult enough as it
> is, I went with Scott’s suggestion and reserved those for an exploratory
